{
  "dom_id": "step-q95352",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\ne A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{T}\\ne B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n${C}^{T}=C$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{169}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{169}[\\begin{aligned}117 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 52\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
